// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文 ext（由 build/passages/ext.txt 生成）
CET6.passages("ext",{"1":{"w":["abound","abusive","accessibility","acclaim","according","accountable","accumulation","accusation","acrobatics","activate","activation","activist","acupuncture","adaptability","adaptive","addiction","adherence","administrator","adorable","adulthood"],"title":"From Acrobat to Healer","t":"As a child, Mei trained in **acrobatics** at a famous circus school in Hebei. She spent her youth balancing on chairs and flying through the air, and audiences across the country **acclaimed|acclaim** her performances. But when she reached **adulthood**, a serious back injury ended her career.\n\nThe years that followed were difficult. She suffered constant pain and developed an **addiction** to painkillers. \"I felt useless,\" she remembers. Her recovery began when a doctor suggested **acupuncture**. After a few months of treatment, the pain eased, and she became fascinated by the method. **According** to her teacher, the needles help **activate** the body's own healing system; scientists describe this **activation** in terms of nerves and blood flow.\n\nMei decided to study traditional medicine. Her training required strict **adherence** to rules, and an **accumulation** of knowledge that took years. Her teachers praised her **adaptability**: she brought the discipline of a performer to her new career and developed an **adaptive** style suited to each patient.\n\nToday she runs a small clinic. Many of her patients are former athletes, and she treats them with special care. Her clinic is designed for **accessibility**, with ramps for wheelchairs and wide doors. Grateful patients **abound**: her waiting room is full from morning till night, and she works closely with a local hospital **administrator** to offer free sessions.\n\nNot everyone supports her work. Online, she sometimes receives **abusive** comments from people who believe acupuncture is nonsense. She once faced an **accusation** that she was promising false cures. Mei says she is **accountable** to her patients and always recommends modern medicine when it is needed. She has become an **activist** for honest health information. Her favourite patients are children, especially an **adorable** six-year-old girl who calls her \"Auntie Needle.\""},"2":{"w":["advancement","advent","advertiser","adviser","advisor","advocacy","aesthetics","affiliation","affirmative","affordability","affordable","afterwards","aging","agitate","agreeableness","ailment","airtime","aisle","akin","alertness"],"title":"The Radio Show for Older Listeners","t":"Every Sunday morning, a community radio station in our city broadcasts a programme called \"Golden Years,\" designed for older listeners. It explores the challenges of **aging** in a society that is changing rapidly. The host, a retired teacher named Mrs. Bai, is famous for her **agreeableness** and warm voice.\n\nThe programme covers practical topics. One week, a financial **adviser** explained how to plan for retirement; another week, a university **advisor** discussed courses for older students. Guests talk about the **affordability** of housing and medicine, and Mrs. Bai often asks officials why more **affordable** care homes are not available.\n\nMrs. Bai is passionate about **advocacy**. She believes that older people deserve respect and the chance for personal **advancement**, even late in life. When a government report suggested reducing pensions, she spoke strongly against it, and her words seemed to **agitate** some politicians. Her answer to criticism is always polite but firm. \"My reply is **affirmative**,\" she once said. \"Yes, we will keep asking questions.\"\n\nThe show has changed with the **advent** of new technology. Listeners can now hear it online, and **advertisers|advertiser** pay for **airtime** to promote health products. Mrs. Bai is careful about her station's **affiliation** with companies, accepting only adverts that are honest and useful.\n\nSome episodes are lighter. One discussed the **aesthetics** of traditional clothing, and another described a common **ailment**: back pain from carrying heavy shopping bags down the supermarket **aisle**. A doctor explained that staying active keeps the mind sharp and improves **alertness**. Mrs. Bai says the show feels **akin** to a family gathering. **Afterwards**, listeners often call to share their own stories."},"3":{"w":["algorithm","alienate","align","allergy","alleviation","alpine","amend","amiable","amnesia","ancestral","ancestry","anew","anguish","animate","animation","anonymity","antagonism","anthropologist","anti","antibiotic"],"title":"The Anthropologist in the Alpine Village","t":"Dr. Ruiz is an **anthropologist** who has spent five years living in an **alpine** village in the Swiss mountains. She studies how small communities preserve their traditions in a modern world. The villagers, who were at first suspicious of outsiders, soon came to see her as an **amiable** neighbour.\n\nHer research focuses on **ancestry** and memory. Many families have lived in the valley for centuries, and their **ancestral** farmhouses are full of old letters and photographs. Some younger villagers use DNA tests, which rely on a computer **algorithm** to estimate where their ancestors came from. Dr. Ruiz is interested in how these results **align** with family stories.\n\nShe has also seen painful moments. One old farmer suffers from **amnesia** after an accident and can no longer remember his childhood. His daughter described her **anguish** at watching him forget. Dr. Ruiz helped the family record his old songs, hoping to bring him some **alleviation** of his loneliness.\n\nThe village faces divisions. Plans for a new ski resort have created **antagonism** between those who want jobs and those who want peace. Some people are strongly **anti**, while others support the project. Dr. Ruiz worries that the conflict may **alienate** neighbours who have been friends for decades. The village council has had to **amend** its rules several times.\n\nVillage life has its lighter side. The local school produced a short **animation** about mountain legends, and children love to **animate** the old stories in drawings. When Dr. Ruiz developed an **allergy** to the hay in her rented barn, the village doctor gave her an **antibiotic** for the infection that followed. She values the **anonymity** of the mountains, where nobody cares about her academic titles. \"Here,\" she says, \"I learn to see the world **anew**.\""},"4":{"w":["anticipation","anymore","ape","app","appal","aptitude","arbitrate","arbor","archaeologist","archaeology","archive","archivist","arena","arguable","arguably","arousal","artery","artifact","artisan","aspirant"],"title":"The Old Arena","t":"In the centre of a small Italian town stands the ruin of a Roman **arena**, where crowds once watched gladiators fight. For **archaeology** students, it is one of the most exciting sites in Europe. Every summer, a team led by a British **archaeologist**, Dr. Hale, spends three months digging beneath its stones.\n\nEach season begins with great **anticipation**. Volunteers hope to find a rare **artifact**: a coin, a piece of jewellery or a tool used by an ancient **artisan**. Last year, the team discovered a carved stone showing an **ape**, which may have been brought from Africa for public shows. The find caused a strong **arousal** of interest in the international press.\n\nDr. Hale keeps careful records. Every object is photographed and stored in a digital **archive**, managed by the team's **archivist**. Students now use a mobile **app** to record their findings in the field, so they don't need paper notebooks **anymore**. \"Technology has changed everything,\" Dr. Hale says. \"**Arguably**, it has changed archaeology more than any discovery.\"\n\nNot all decisions are easy. When two universities disputed who should study a newly found statue, a professor was invited to **arbitrate** between them. Some theories about the site remain **arguable**: historians disagree about how many people the arena could hold. Dr. Hale was **appalled|appal** when tourists tried to take stones home as souvenirs.\n\nThe town has embraced the project. A wooden **arbor** covered with vines provides shade for workers, and local families bring food at lunchtime. The main road to the site, once a busy **artery** for trade, is now lined with cafés. Dr. Hale looks for one quality in every young **aspirant**: not just an **aptitude** for science, but patience. \"The past,\" she says, \"does not like to be rushed.\""},"5":{"w":["aspirational","aspire","assertion","assortment","astronomical","attainable","attendee","attic","auction","aura","auspicious","authorship","autonomy","aversion","avoidance","awaken","awareness","backlash","backyard","bacterial"],"title":"Treasures in the Attic","t":"When my grandmother died last year, we found an old wooden box in her **attic**. It contained an **assortment** of strange objects: letters, coins, silk scarves and a small painting of a mountain at dawn. The painting had no signature, and its **authorship** became a family mystery.\n\nMy cousin, who works at a museum, took the painting to an expert. After weeks of research, the expert made a surprising **assertion**: it might be the work of a famous nineteenth-century artist. The family decided to sell it at **auction**, hoping the money could pay for my younger sister's education.\n\nThe auction day felt **auspicious**. The room was full, and each **attendee** seemed excited. The painting had a special **aura**; it glowed under the lights. Bidding began slowly but quickly rose to **astronomical** levels. In the end, it sold for more than a million yuan.\n\nThe sale caused an unexpected **backlash**. Some relatives felt we should have kept the painting, and others accused us of greed. My mother, who has a strong **aversion** to conflict, chose **avoidance** and stopped answering phone calls for a week. Eventually, the family met in her **backyard** to talk things through.\n\nThe experience seemed to **awaken** something in all of us. We gained a new **awareness** of my grandmother's life; her letters showed that she had once longed to become an artist herself. We also discovered why the paper was damaged: **bacterial** growth from damp air in the attic. My sister now **aspires|aspire** to study art history. Her dream, once just **aspirational**, now feels **attainable**. My grandmother always valued **autonomy**, and we think she would be happy that her treasure gave a young woman the freedom to choose her own path."},"6":{"w":["bail","baseline","behavioral","behaviorally","beholder","benefaction","beverage","bike-sharing","bilingual","bilingualism","bio","biodiversity","biological","biologically","biologist","blender","blog","bloody","bolster","borrower"],"title":"The City Biologist","t":"Dr. Ma is a **biologist** who studies wildlife in the middle of a busy city. Her popular **blog**, \"Wild Streets,\" describes the birds, insects and small animals that live among skyscrapers and shopping centres. Her short **bio** on the website says simply: \"I look for nature where nobody expects it.\"\n\nHer research focuses on urban **biodiversity**. Five years ago, she recorded a **baseline** count of species in the city's parks, and she now measures changes every year. She is especially interested in **behavioral** adaptation: how animals change their habits to survive near humans. Some birds, she found, are **behaviorally** different from their countryside relatives; they sing louder to be heard over traffic.\n\nHer work has practical uses. Data from her surveys helped **bolster** a campaign to protect green spaces from development. A wealthy businessman, impressed by her findings, made a generous **benefaction** to fund a new nature reserve. Dr. Ma writes every post in both Chinese and English, and she believes **bilingualism** helps her reach a global audience. Her own children are **bilingual**, too.\n\nShe travels everywhere by **bike-sharing**, collecting samples as she rides. Last year, the bike company almost went bankrupt, and the city had to **bail** it out with public money. Dr. Ma once fell off a bike and got a **bloody** nose, but she laughed it off and kept working.\n\nDr. Ma also writes about food. She explains the **biological** effects of sugary **beverages|beverage** and shares recipes for healthy smoothies made in a **blender**. \"**Biologically**, we are designed to love sweetness,\" she writes, \"but too much is harmful.\" She once lent her favourite camera to a student, and the **borrower** returned it with photos of a rare owl. \"Beauty,\" she says, \"is in the eye of the **beholder**. In the city, you just have to look harder.\""},"7":{"w":["botanist","bother","bracket","breach","breastfeed","brew","buffer","builder","buildup","burger","calligraphy","calorie","canyon","capitalize","carbohydrate","carer","cashless","catastrophic","categorize","catwalk"],"title":"Into the Canyon","t":"Last autumn, a **botanist** named Dr. Wen led a small team into a deep **canyon** in the mountains of Yunnan. Their goal was to **categorize** the plants growing on its steep walls, many of which had never been studied. The only way across the river was a narrow metal **catwalk** built by local farmers decades ago.\n\nThe trip required careful planning. Each member carried enough food for ten days, and Dr. Wen calculated every **calorie**. Meals were rich in **carbohydrates|carbohydrate** such as rice and noodles, though one volunteer joked that he would give anything for a **burger**. Each evening, the team would **brew** strong tea over a campfire.\n\nThe canyon's ecology is fragile. Dense forests on the upper slopes act as a **buffer**, protecting the valley from heavy rain. But logging upstream has caused a **buildup** of mud in the river, and a single storm could be **catastrophic**. Dr. Wen worries that some developers **capitalize** on weak rules and are in **breach** of environmental laws.\n\nThe team met local villagers along the way. A young mother, who was about to **breastfeed** her baby, invited them into her home and offered them food. An elderly **carer** looked after her disabled brother. A village **builder** explained how houses were constructed without nails. Even in this remote area, people used phones to pay for everything, and markets were almost completely **cashless**.\n\nDr. Wen recorded her notes in beautiful **calligraphy**, a hobby she has practised since childhood. She grouped plants by height, writing each **bracket** of measurements carefully in her book. \"Some people ask why I **bother** with such slow work,\" she says. \"But every plant matters.\""},"8":{"w":["causation","celebrity","center","chaotic","charitable","cheeseburger","chemotherapy","chihuahua","childbearing","childbirth","chimpanzee","chore","chromosome","chunk","citizenship","civic","clarity","clause","clench","clog"],"title":"The Scientist on Television","t":"Professor Zhou is a geneticist who has become an unlikely **celebrity**. Her weekly television show explains science in simple language, and millions of viewers watch it. In one popular episode, she showed how humans and the **chimpanzee** share almost ninety-nine percent of their DNA, using a model of a **chromosome** made of coloured balls.\n\nHer research focuses on **causation** in disease. She studies how genes and lifestyle together affect health, especially during **childbearing** and **childbirth**. \"Correlation is not **causation**,\" she often reminds her audience, explaining that two things happening together does not prove one caused the other.\n\nProfessor Zhou is also deeply **charitable**. She runs a foundation that supports patients at a cancer **center** in her hometown, many of whom are undergoing **chemotherapy**. She visits them regularly, bringing books and her small **chihuahua**, which the patients love. She says her mother's illness inspired her to study genetics.\n\nHer life is often **chaotic**. Between research, filming and travel, she rarely has time for household **chores|chore**, and she admits that her kitchen is a disaster. Her favourite treat after a long day is a **cheeseburger**, which she eats in her car. A large **chunk** of her income goes to her foundation.\n\nShe believes that science is a **civic** duty. Good **citizenship**, she argues, requires understanding the basic facts behind public decisions. When a television company once tried to add a **clause** to her contract limiting what she could say about food companies, she refused to sign. \"**Clarity** matters more than money,\" she said, her hands **clenched|clench** in frustration. She warns that misinformation can **clog** public debate like rubbish in a drain."},"9":{"w":["cognition","collaborative","collaborator","collectivism","collectivistic","collector","colonize","colorful","comet","commemorate","communicator","commuter","compassion","compassionate","competency","competitiveness","complacent","complementary","condemnation","conditioner"],"title":"Two Ways of Thinking","t":"At a university in Shanghai, a psychology professor named Dr. Lam studies how culture shapes human **cognition**. Her research compares societies influenced by **collectivism**, where group harmony matters most, with those that value individual freedom. \"People in **collectivistic** cultures often notice relationships,\" she explains, \"while others focus on individual objects.\"\n\nHer work is highly **collaborative**. She leads a team of researchers from twelve countries, and each **collaborator** brings a different perspective. The project is a **colorful** mix of languages and ideas. Dr. Lam is an excellent **communicator**, able to explain complex theories in simple words.\n\nOne study examined how people behave on crowded trains. Researchers interviewed every kind of **commuter**, from students to office workers, about how they react when someone needs help. Those who showed the most **compassion** were not always the richest or best educated. Dr. Lam believes that **compassionate** behaviour is learned, not born.\n\nHer findings challenge some common beliefs. Many schools focus on **competitiveness**, but Dr. Lam argues that cooperation is an equally important **competency**. She warns that wealthy societies can become **complacent**, assuming their ways are best. She believes that different cultural values are **complementary** rather than opposed.\n\nNot everyone agrees. Some critics have expressed strong **condemnation** of her methods, claiming that cultural categories are too simple. Dr. Lam welcomes the debate. Outside work, she is a keen **collector** of old maps and once watched a **comet** from a mountain top. She plans a ceremony to **commemorate** her mentor, who taught her that ideas, like moss, slowly **colonize** new ground. Even the broken air **conditioner** in her office does not bother her. \"Science needs patience,\" she says, \"and a good fan.\""},"10":{"w":["conducive","confide","confucianism","congestion","conjure","connotation","conscientious","conservationist","conservatism","constructive","consultation","consumerism","contagious","contemplation","contentious","convergence","conveyor","cooperation","copyright","cornerstone"],"title":"The Old Teacher's Lessons","t":"In a quiet courtyard in Qufu, the hometown of Confucius, an elderly teacher named Mr. Kong runs evening classes on **Confucianism**. His students include office workers, retirees and university students, all looking for a moment of **contemplation** away from the noise and **congestion** of modern cities. The courtyard is peaceful, an atmosphere **conducive** to quiet thought.\n\nMr. Kong is a **conscientious** teacher who prepares every lesson carefully. He explains the **connotation** of ancient words, showing how their meanings have changed over time. He believes Confucian ideas are the **cornerstone** of Chinese culture, but he rejects blind **conservatism**. \"Tradition must be living,\" he says. \"Otherwise it becomes a museum.\"\n\nHis classes encourage **constructive** debate. Some topics are **contentious**: students argue about the role of women in traditional thought, or whether ancient values can survive modern **consumerism**. Mr. Kong listens patiently and offers advice in private **consultation** when students **confide** their personal problems to him.\n\nHe sees a **convergence** between Eastern and Western ideas. Both traditions, he argues, value honesty, learning and **cooperation**. One student, who works on a factory **conveyor** line, said the classes helped him see meaning in his repetitive job. Another, a **conservationist**, connected Confucian ideas about harmony with nature to her environmental work.\n\nMr. Kong's enthusiasm is **contagious**. His stories **conjure** vivid images of ancient scholars travelling between kingdoms. A publisher recently offered to buy the **copyright** to his lecture notes, but he refused. \"Knowledge should be shared freely,\" he says, smiling."},"11":{"w":["cosmetic","counterfeit","counterproductive","countless","couplet","courier","coverage","crackdown","craftsman","craftsmanship","cram","crave","creation","creativity","credibility","credible","crumble","culinary","cult","cultivation"],"title":"The Brush Maker","t":"In an old lane in Huzhou, a **craftsman** named Mr. Shen makes calligraphy brushes by hand, as his family has done for five generations. Each brush takes days to finish, and his **craftsmanship** is admired by artists across the country. Before every Spring Festival, he writes red **couplets|couplet** for his neighbours with his own brushes.\n\nHis workshop is small and **crammed|cram** with materials: bamboo handles, bundles of goat hair and jars of glue. The bamboo comes from his own fields, where careful **cultivation** produces straight, strong stems. \"Every brush is a **creation**,\" he says. \"It must have a soul.\"\n\nThe business faces many challenges. Cheap **counterfeit** brushes, sold online with fake labels, have damaged the reputation of real Huzhou brushes. Last year, the government launched a **crackdown** on fake products, and the media **coverage** helped consumers recognise genuine ones. Mr. Shen says that lowering his prices would be **counterproductive**; customers who **crave** quality are willing to pay for it.\n\nHe has adapted to modern life. Orders now arrive online, and a **courier** collects parcels from his door every afternoon. His daughter, a chef who runs a **culinary** school, designed the shop's website. She says that **creativity** in marketing is just as important as skill. The brushes have even developed a **cult** following among young artists on social media.\n\nMr. Shen is proud of his **credibility**. His customers trust him because he never exaggerates. When a journalist once asked whether his brushes really last longer, he gave a simple, **credible** answer: \"Use one for a year and see.\" He refuses to make **cosmetic** changes just to follow fashion. His old house is beginning to **crumble**, but he says it has witnessed **countless** brushes and happy customers, and he will never leave."},"12":{"w":["cultural","culturally","curtail","cyberloafing","cyborg","dataset","daytime","deadlock","decease","dedication","defensive","defer","deforest","defuse","delta","delusional","dementia","democrat","demographic","dependable"],"title":"The Data Scientist","t":"Dr. Liu is a data scientist who studies workplace behaviour. Her latest research examines **cyberloafing**: the habit of browsing the internet for personal reasons during working hours. Using a large **dataset** from two hundred companies, she found that almost every employee does it, especially during the **daytime** hours after lunch.\n\nHer findings surprised many managers. Some wanted to **curtail** internet access completely, but Dr. Liu argues that short breaks can actually improve productivity. Managers who become **defensive** about the issue, she says, often create more stress. She also found **cultural** differences: workers in some countries feel guiltier about breaks than others, and attitudes are **culturally** shaped.\n\nHer work has practical uses. In one company, talks between managers and workers reached a **deadlock** over a new monitoring system. Dr. Liu was invited to **defuse** the situation, and she suggested a compromise that both sides accepted. She later agreed to **defer** publishing the results until the company had time to review them.\n\nDr. Liu's interest in human behaviour began at home. Her grandmother, who suffers from **dementia**, often forgets names but remembers songs from childhood. Dr. Liu says caring for her taught her **dedication** and patience. Her family comes from the Pearl River **Delta**, and her grandmother tells stories of her **deceased|decease** husband, a farmer who helped **deforest** hills to grow rice.\n\nShe also studies the **demographic** changes in China's workforce. Older workers, she says, are more **dependable** than many employers assume. Some critics call her ideas **delusional**, but she ignores them. She jokes that one day we will all become a **cyborg**, half human and half machine. A colleague, a committed **democrat**, supports her proposal to let employees vote on workplace rules."},"13":{"w":["dependence","depiction","deplete","deplorable","deplore","designer","desolation","detachment","deter","detrimental","devastate","developer","developmental","diabetes","diagnosis","dietary","differentiate","diffusion","digestive","digit"],"title":"The Game Designer's Diagnosis","t":"At twenty-eight, Zhang Hao was a successful game **designer** working for a large technology company. He spent long hours in front of a screen, surviving on coffee, sweets and instant noodles. Then, during a routine health check, he received a shocking **diagnosis**: type 2 **diabetes**.\n\nThe news seemed to **devastate** him at first. \"I thought diabetes was a disease of old people,\" he says. His doctor explained that poor **dietary** habits and lack of exercise can be **detrimental** to anyone's health. She warned that his **dependence** on sugar had begun to **deplete** his body's ability to control blood sugar, and his **digestive** system was also affected.\n\nHao decided to change his life. He began cooking his own meals and walking to work. He learned to **differentiate** between healthy and unhealthy carbohydrates and checked every **digit** on his blood test reports. \"Numbers became my friends,\" he jokes.\n\nHis experience also changed his work. As a game **developer**, he designed a new mobile game that encourages players to exercise. Its **depiction** of a desert island, full of **desolation** until the player brings it back to life by walking, became surprisingly popular. Child psychologists praised its **developmental** benefits for young players.\n\nHao is now an advocate for healthier office culture. He criticises the **deplorable** working conditions at some technology companies and openly **deplores|deplore** the habit of rewarding long hours. He believes that companies should **deter** unhealthy behaviour rather than encourage it. He has learned to view his illness with calm **detachment**. \"The **diffusion** of good habits takes time,\" he says, \"but one game at a time, it can happen.\""},"14":{"w":["digitization","diner","diplomacy","diplomat","directive","directness","disbelieve","disciple","discomfort","disconnect","discredit","discreet","discriminate","dishearten","disparity","dissertation","distraction","diversity","doctoral","doctorate"],"title":"A Dissertation on Diplomacy","t":"Last year, my friend Yue completed her **doctoral** studies in history. Her **dissertation** examined how Chinese **diplomacy** developed during the nineteenth century, based on thousands of letters written by a forgotten **diplomat**. When she received her **doctorate**, her parents seemed to **disbelieve** the news at first; she was the first in her family to attend university.\n\nHer research depended on **digitization**. A national archive had recently scanned millions of old documents, making them available online. Yue spent months reading letters, reports and official **directives|directive** from the imperial court. She was struck by the diplomat's **directness**: unlike many officials of his time, he wrote honestly about China's weaknesses.\n\nThe diplomat's life was difficult. His enemies tried to **discredit** him by spreading rumours, and he was often **disheartened|dishearten** by the court's refusal to listen. He was a **disciple** of a reformist scholar, and he believed that China should learn from other nations. In private letters, he was **discreet**, using codes to protect his ideas.\n\nYue's research also revealed the **disparity** between official records and private letters. Officials often described meetings as friendly, while private notes showed great **discomfort** and tension. She found evidence that foreign governments would **discriminate** against Chinese merchants in trade agreements.\n\nWriting the dissertation was exhausting. Yue had to **disconnect** from social media, which she called her greatest **distraction**. She often worked in a small **diner** near the university, drinking tea late into the night. She now teaches history and encourages **diversity** of opinion in her classes. \"History is never simple,\" she tells her students."},"15":{"w":["dolphin","domesticate","dominance","donation","doodle","doze","drone","droplet","dual","dub","duckling","duplication","dweller","dysfunction","e-commerce","eagerness","earner","eater","echolocation","ecological"],"title":"Listening to Dolphins","t":"Dr. Ng is a marine biologist who studies **dolphins|dolphin** off the coast of Hong Kong. Her research focuses on **echolocation**, the system dolphins use to find food by sending out sounds and listening to the echoes. \"It's like seeing with your ears,\" she explains to visitors.\n\nHer team uses modern technology. A small **drone** flies above the water, filming dolphin groups from the air without disturbing them. The camera can even capture the tiny **droplets|droplet** of water that dolphins breathe out. The team then studies the videos to understand social **dominance** within each group.\n\nThe work has an **ecological** purpose. Busy shipping lanes, pollution and **e-commerce** deliveries by boat have increased noise in the sea, causing **dysfunction** in the dolphins' ability to hunt. Dr. Ng's team has found that some groups are moving away from areas where they have lived for generations, much like a city **dweller** escaping a noisy street.\n\nFunding comes partly from public **donations|donation**. Dr. Ng also serves a **dual** role as researcher and teacher, giving talks in schools. Children's **eagerness** to learn always inspires her. One boy drew a **doodle** of a dolphin wearing headphones, which she now uses as the project's logo. Journalists have **dubbed|dub** her \"the dolphin whisperer.\"\n\nShe often explains how humans **domesticate** animals, while dolphins remain wild and free. Though her salary is modest compared with other high **earners|earner** in the city, she says the job is priceless. After long days at sea, she sometimes falls into a **doze** on the boat. She warns against unnecessary **duplication** of research, and she shares all her data with other scientists. At home, she keeps a small pond for a **duckling** she rescued from a storm drain, and although she is a fussy **eater**, she never refuses fresh fish."},"16":{"w":["economist","educational","effectiveness","effortful","effortlessly","electrification","electrify","embodiment","emergence","empower","emulate","enact","encyclopedia","endorse","enhancement","enjoyment","enlightenment","enrage","enthusiast","entitlement"],"title":"Lights in the Mountains","t":"Twenty years ago, my grandfather's village in Guizhou had no electricity. Families cooked over wood fires and read by oil lamps. Then a government programme brought **electrification** to the region, and the arrival of power lines seemed to **electrify** the whole community. My grandfather still remembers the night the first bulb lit up: \"Everyone cheered as if it were the Spring Festival.\"\n\nAn **economist** who studied the programme later described it as one of the most important changes in rural China. Electricity allowed the **emergence** of small businesses, from rice mills to repair shops. It also had **educational** benefits: children could study at night, and the school received its first computer. The village's only **encyclopedia**, once read by candlelight, was soon replaced by the internet.\n\nMeasuring the programme's **effectiveness** was not simple. Some villagers adapted **effortlessly**, buying televisions and fridges within months. For others, especially the elderly, learning new technology was **effortful**. My grandmother, a keen gardening **enthusiast**, refused to use the electric cooker for years.\n\nThe changes helped to **empower** women in particular. With electric machines, they spent less time on household tasks and more on paid work. Officials began to **enact** new policies encouraging women to start businesses, and several famous entrepreneurs agreed to **endorse** the programme. One woman who opened a tea shop became the **embodiment** of the village's new spirit, and others tried to **emulate** her success.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. A rise in electricity prices once seemed to **enrage** villagers, who argued that cheap power was an **entitlement**, not a privilege. But over time, most people agreed that life had improved. The **enhancement** of daily comfort brought new **enjoyment**: films, music and long evenings with friends. For my grandfather, the biggest change was a kind of **enlightenment**. \"We could finally see the world,\" he says."},"17":{"w":["entrepreneurial","environmentalism","environmentalist","envoy","enzyme","epic","equinox","equity","erasure","erode","escalate","esteem","ethic","etiquette","euro","evacuation","evade","eventual","excerpt","exclusion"],"title":"The Climate Summit","t":"Last spring, a major climate conference was held in Brussels, attracting leaders, scientists and activists from around the world. Each government sent an **envoy**, and the meetings followed strict diplomatic **etiquette**. For two weeks, the city was full of journalists, protesters and police.\n\nThe talks began on the spring **equinox**, a date chosen to symbolise balance between rich and poor nations. **Equity** was a central issue: developing countries argued that wealthy nations should pay more, since they had produced most of the pollution. The cost of new programmes was estimated in billions of **euros|euro**.\n\nTensions began to **escalate** as negotiations continued. Some countries tried to **evade** responsibility for cutting emissions, while others threatened to walk out. The **exclusion** of small island nations from one meeting caused anger, and an **environmentalist** from the Pacific gave an emotional speech. An **excerpt** from her speech was shared millions of times online: \"Our islands are disappearing while you argue.\"\n\nThe conference also highlighted new ideas. An **entrepreneurial** young scientist presented an **enzyme** that can break down plastic in days rather than centuries. Others spoke about how rising seas **erode** coastlines and force the **evacuation** of entire communities. One historian warned against the **erasure** of indigenous knowledge about nature.\n\nThe **eventual** agreement was not perfect, but many saw it as an **epic** achievement. Observers held the chief negotiator in high **esteem** for her patience. She said that **environmentalism** must be guided by a strong **ethic** of fairness. \"This is not the end,\" she said. \"It is the beginning.\""},"18":{"w":["exclusivity","exemption","existential","expectancy","expectant","experimenter","exploratory","expressive","expressway","fabulous","factual","famed","fantasize","farmland","fascist","feasibility","feat","feebleness","femininity","fertility"],"title":"The Road Through the Farmland","t":"In a small valley in northern Italy, the government planned to build a new **expressway** through the middle of the region's best **farmland**. Officials argued that the road would cut travel times in half. But local farmers, many of whose families had worked the land for centuries, strongly opposed the plan.\n\nThe farmers hired independent experts to study the project's **feasibility**. Their **exploratory** report, led by a soil scientist who called herself \"an **experimenter** in the service of farmers,\" questioned the government's claims, presenting **factual** evidence that the road would damage the soil's **fertility** and pollute nearby rivers. The report also noted that the region is **famed** for its wine and cheese, which depend on clean land.\n\nThe debate had deep historical roots. The village had suffered under **fascist** rule in the 1930s, when land was taken from families without compensation. Older residents remembered stories of that time, and they feared history might repeat itself. A local teacher said that the conflict had an **existential** dimension: \"If we lose the land, we lose who we are.\"\n\nThe campaign was led by a remarkable woman, an eighty-year-old farmer known for her **expressive** speeches. Despite the **feebleness** of her body, her voice was strong. Journalists praised her courage, calling her campaign a **fabulous** example of community action. She rejected old ideas about **femininity**, saying that women had always been the strength of the countryside.\n\nEventually, the government agreed to change the route. The decision was celebrated as a great **feat**. The farmers did not seek **exclusivity** over the land or any tax **exemption**; they simply wanted to protect it. Young couples in the village, including an **expectant** mother who joined every protest, now **fantasize** about a peaceful future. Life **expectancy** in the valley is among the highest in Italy, and residents joke that clean air is the secret."},"19":{"w":["feudal","fewer","filial","finder","fingertip","firecracker","flavor","flexibility","flip","folklore","footprint","footwear","foreseeable","forestry","forgo","founder","frantic","frontline","frugality","frustration"],"title":"The Founder Returns Home","t":"Mr. Ou is the **founder** of a successful **footwear** company that sells shoes all over the world. Last winter, after twenty years in the city, he returned to his home village for the Spring Festival. He found a place that had changed, but whose **folklore** and traditions remained strong.\n\nOn New Year's Eve, the village was **frantic** with preparations. Children ran through the lanes, lighting a **firecracker** at every corner, while women prepared dumplings with every **flavor** imaginable. Old men told stories from **feudal** times, when landlords controlled the valley and farmers lived in poverty.\n\nMr. Ou was moved by the villagers' **frugality**. Many families had **fewer** possessions than his office staff, yet they were generous with food and time. He noticed how young people still showed **filial** respect to their parents, bowing to them on New Year's morning. \"In the city, we often **forgo** these traditions,\" he said. \"We are too busy.\"\n\nThe visit also revealed problems. The village's **forestry** jobs had disappeared, and many young people had left. Mr. Ou felt a deep **frustration** at the lack of opportunities. He decided to open a small factory in the village, bringing jobs back home. \"In the **foreseeable** future,\" he said, \"I want our village to be a place where young people can stay.\"\n\nHe is also concerned about the environment. His company has reduced its carbon **footprint** by using recycled materials. Mr. Ou gives his workers great **flexibility** in their hours, especially those with children. During the festival, he liked to **flip** through old photo albums with his mother, touching each picture with his **fingertip**. Before leaving, he donated money to health workers on the **frontline** of rural medicine. On his last day, he dropped his wallet in the market, and its **finder**, a small boy, ran after him to return it. Mr. Ou says that honesty is still the village's greatest treasure."},"20":{"w":["fulfillment","functional","futile","gadget","gassy","gender","generational","genre","geographic","geological","giver","glacier","glamorous","globalization","glutinous","gosh","governance","governmental","grader","graphic"],"title":"Drawing the Glaciers","t":"Lin Xue is a young artist who creates **graphic** novels about climate change. Her latest book tells the story of a **glacier** in Tibet that is slowly melting. It mixes science with fantasy, a **genre** that has become increasingly popular among young readers. \"I want to make science feel alive,\" she says.\n\nHer research took her to remote mountains, where she interviewed scientists studying the **geological** history of the region. They explained how **geographic** factors, such as altitude and wind patterns, affect ice formation. She found the work fascinating but sad. \"Trying to stop the melting feels **futile** sometimes,\" she admits, \"but we must try.\"\n\nThe book also explores **generational** differences. In one chapter, a grandmother and her granddaughter argue about **globalization**: the grandmother remembers a simple life, while the girl loves every new **gadget**. Lin Xue says she wanted to show both sides without judgement. The book also challenges traditional ideas about **gender**, featuring a female scientist as the hero.\n\nNot all readers are adults. A fifth-**grader** wrote to Lin Xue, saying the book had inspired her to become a scientist. \"**Gosh**, I cried when I read that letter,\" Lin Xue laughs. She believes that artists can be generous **givers|giver** of hope.\n\nHer life is less **glamorous** than people imagine. She works in a small, **functional** studio and lives on simple meals. Her favourite food is **glutinous** rice dumplings, which her mother sends from home, and she avoids **gassy** drinks because they give her headaches. She has opinions on politics too: she argues that better **governance** and stronger **governmental** action are needed to protect the environment. For her, creating art brings a deep sense of **fulfillment**. \"Each page is a small gift to the future,\" she says."},"21":{"w":["grassland","gravitate","grid","groundnut","guideline","gut","habitat","hack","halfway","hallway","handicraft","harass","harassment","haven","havoc","haze","hazy","headphone","healthcare","hearted"],"title":"Guardian of the Grassland","t":"Mr. Bayar is a ranger on the vast **grassland** of Inner Mongolia, where wild horses, gazelles and countless birds make their home. His job is to protect their **habitat** from overgrazing, illegal hunting and careless tourists. For many species, the protected area is the last safe **haven** in the region.\n\nHis work follows strict **guidelines|guideline**. Each spring, he divides the reserve into a **grid** of squares and counts the animals in each one. He travels by motorbike, often riding for hours to reach the most remote areas. Sometimes he must **hack** a path through thick bushes near the rivers. When he is **halfway** through a long patrol, he stops at a herder's tent for tea.\n\nThe grassland faces many threats. In recent years, dust storms have caused **havoc**, burying roads and damaging crops. On some days, a thick **haze** covers the sky, and the horizon becomes **hazy** and grey. Tourists sometimes **harass** animals to take photos, and Mr. Bayar has reported several cases of **harassment** to the police. Once, a group of young men even tried to **hack** into the reserve's website to find the location of rare birds.\n\nMr. Bayar trusts his **gut** when something seems wrong. \"Animals tell you a lot,\" he says. \"If birds suddenly fly away, someone is nearby.\" He wears **headphones|headphone** to listen to bird calls recorded by sensors placed across the reserve.\n\nHe is a **kind-hearted|hearted** man, admired by local herders. Their wives sell **handicraft** goods to tourists, and he helps them find buyers. He also supports better **healthcare** for herder families, many of whom live far from hospitals. In the evenings, children **gravitate** towards his tent to hear stories, and he shares a bag of roasted **groundnuts|groundnut** with them. His small house has a long **hallway** decorated with photos of every animal he has ever rescued."},"22":{"w":["hectare","herbal","herbicide","hierarchical","high-speed","hmm","holder","hologram","homecoming","homeless","homelessness","homeowner","honor","honorable","horrify","hostility","houseplant","humanities","hybrid","hydroelectric"],"title":"Homecoming","t":"Last spring, my cousin Wei took a **high-speed** train back to his hometown for the first time in ten years. The journey that once took two days now took only five hours. His **homecoming** was emotional: his elderly parents waited at the station, holding a sign with his name.\n\nThe town had changed beyond recognition. A new **hydroelectric** dam supplied power to the whole region, and rows of modern houses lined the river. Many farmers had become **homeowners|homeowner** for the first time, buying flats with money earned in the cities. Wei's father now farms just two **hectares|hectare** of land, growing a **hybrid** rice that produces much higher yields.\n\nBut not everything was better. Wei was **horrified|horrify** to learn that some farmers used too much **herbicide**, which was poisoning fish in the river. His mother, who still relies on **herbal** remedies, worried about the water. Wei also noticed **homeless** men sleeping under the new bridge, a sight he had never seen in his childhood. \"**Homelessness** has come with wealth,\" his father said quietly.\n\nWei studies the **humanities** at a university in Beijing, and he is interested in how social structures change. He observed that the town's old **hierarchical** system, in which elders made every decision, was weakening. Some young people showed open **hostility** towards traditional leaders, while others still treated them with **honor**.\n\nAt a family dinner, his uncle asked what he planned to do after graduation. \"**Hmm**,\" Wei said, thinking carefully. \"Maybe I'll come back and teach.\" His uncle smiled and called it an **honorable** choice. Before leaving, Wei bought his mother a **houseplant** and a phone **holder** for her kitchen. His younger nephew showed him a toy that projected a **hologram** of a dinosaur. \"Some things change fast,\" Wei laughed."},"23":{"w":["hydropower","hygiene","hype","hypocritical","hypothesize","iconic","idealise","idealistic","idol","immersive","immoral","imperfection","impersonal","implant","implementation","impractical","imprint","inability","inadequacy","inappropriate"],"title":"The Museum of Water","t":"Last year, a young engineer named Xiao Fang designed an **immersive** museum about **hydropower** in her hometown. Visitors walk through dark rooms where projections show rivers flowing, dams rising and turbines spinning. The experience is so realistic that children often reach out to touch the water.\n\nXiao Fang has always been **idealistic**. As a student, she dreamed of using clean energy to change the world. Her friends sometimes called her plans **impractical**, but she ignored them. \"If you don't **idealise** the future a little,\" she says, \"you'll never try to build it.\"\n\nThe museum became a sensation after a famous pop **idol** visited and posted photos online. The **hype** was enormous: thousands of fans arrived the next weekend. Some critics called the celebrity **hypocritical**, pointing out that he flew everywhere by private jet. Xiao Fang refused to comment, saying it would be **inappropriate** to judge him.\n\nThe museum also addresses difficult questions. One room shows how dams can displace communities, and visitors are asked whether forcing people to move is **immoral**. Another explains the importance of clean water and **hygiene** in developing countries. Xiao Fang wanted to show both the benefits and the **imperfection** of every technology.\n\nRunning the museum was harder than designing it. The **implementation** of her plans was slowed by an **impersonal** bureaucracy, and she often felt frustrated by her **inability** to raise enough money. She admits feeling a sense of **inadequacy** at times. A scientist friend helped her **hypothesize** how visitors learn best, and they tested ideas together. The museum's glass dome has become an **iconic** building in the city. Xiao Fang hopes the experience will leave a lasting **imprint** on young visitors and **implant** the idea that clean energy is possible."},"24":{"w":["inborn","inbox","incidental","inclusion","incomprehensible","inconsistency","inconsistent","inconvenience","indecision","indicator","indifference","indigenous","individualism","individualistic","individuality","indulgence","industrialization","ineffective","inequality","inequitable"],"title":"Two Ways of Life","t":"Dr. Tan is an anthropologist who studies an **indigenous** community living in the mountains of Taiwan. For decades, its people lived by farming and hunting, but rapid **industrialization** has transformed the region. Factories and roads have brought jobs, but also new problems.\n\nHer research compares two value systems. Traditional village life emphasises community, while modern city life encourages **individualism**. Young people who move to cities often become more **individualistic**, valuing personal freedom and **individuality** over family duty. Dr. Tan avoids judging either way of life. \"Each has strengths and weaknesses,\" she says.\n\nShe has observed growing **inequality** between those who benefit from development and those who do not. Land has been distributed in an **inequitable** way, and some families have lost their fields. Government programmes to help them have often been **ineffective**, partly because officials show **indifference** to local needs. One important **indicator** of the problem is the rising number of young people leaving school early.\n\nHer work faces challenges. Some officials find her reports **incomprehensible** because they are full of academic language, and she is trying to write more clearly. She also found **inconsistency** in government data: population figures were **inconsistent** across different reports. Gathering information can be slow, and she apologises to villagers for any **inconvenience** her visits cause.\n\nDr. Tan believes in the **inclusion** of local voices in every decision. She argues that a sense of fairness is **inborn** in all humans, and that development should never be an **indulgence** for the rich alone. Her **inbox** is full of messages from students who want to join her research. At present, the community is caught in **indecision** about whether to accept a new factory: the jobs are far from **incidental** to people's survival, but neither is their land. \"There is no simple answer,\" she says."},"25":{"w":["inequity","inevitability","infection","informative","infrequent","infringe","infringement","inheritance","inhibition","injustice","innovate","innovative","innovator","insecure","insecurity","inspirational","instinctive","institutional","instructive","insulation"],"title":"The Insulation Inventor","t":"At only twenty-four, Zhou Ning has become one of China's most celebrated young **innovators|innovator**. She invented a cheap form of **insulation** made from straw and recycled paper, which keeps rural homes warm in winter. Her story has become **inspirational** to students across the country.\n\nZhou grew up in a poor village in the north, where winters are bitterly cold. Many families could not afford heating, and children often caught a serious **infection** during the coldest months. She felt a deep sense of **injustice** at this **inequity**. \"Rich families lived comfortably while poor families froze,\" she remembers.\n\nHer decision to **innovate** came from an **instinctive** desire to help. As a university student, she spent months testing materials in her dormitory. Most experiments failed, but she found them **instructive**. When one design finally worked, she felt that success was not luck but an **inevitability** of hard work.\n\nHer **innovative** product soon attracted attention. However, a large company copied her design without permission, an **infringement** of her patent. Zhou took the company to court, arguing that it had tried to **infringe** her rights. She won the case, though the process was stressful. She admits that she sometimes feels **insecure** speaking in public, and she still struggles with **insecurity** about her abilities.\n\nZhou believes that **institutional** support is essential for young inventors. She criticises the **infrequent** funding for rural research and gives **informative** talks at universities about intellectual property. Her small **inheritance** from her grandmother paid for her first experiments. \"My grandmother taught me to overcome my **inhibition**,\" she says. \"She told me never to be afraid of trying.\""},"26":{"w":["intangible","integration","interactive","interdependence","internship","interpersonal","interviewee","interviewer","intolerance","introvert","intrusion","intuitive","invasive","investigator","investor","involuntary","involvement","irrational","irrationally","irrelevant"],"title":"The Summer Internship","t":"Last summer, my friend Rui, a shy **introvert** who studies biology, applied for an **internship** at a national park. The interview terrified her. The **interviewer** asked difficult questions about teamwork and **interpersonal** skills, and Rui felt like the weakest **interviewee** in the room. To her surprise, she got the job.\n\nHer project focused on an **invasive** plant species that was spreading rapidly through the park's wetlands. She worked with a senior **investigator** who had studied the problem for years. Rui discovered that she had an **intuitive** understanding of plant behaviour; she could often guess where the weed would appear next. Her supervisor praised her careful **involvement** in every stage of the research.\n\nThe work showed her the **interdependence** of living things. When the invasive plant took over, native insects disappeared, and birds lost their food. Rui helped design an **interactive** map that allowed visitors to see the changes over time. The park hoped the map would support better **integration** of science and public education.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the project. Some local farmers saw the researchers' visits as an **intrusion** on their land, and one wealthy **investor**, who planned to build a hotel nearby, argued that the weed was **irrelevant** to tourism. Rui found it hard to understand why people reacted so **irrationally** to scientific evidence. \"Fear makes people **irrational**,\" her supervisor explained.\n\nThe internship also taught Rui about culture. Local elders shared songs and stories, part of the region's **intangible** heritage. At one dinner, Rui politely refused milk tea because of her lactose **intolerance**, and an elder let out an **involuntary** laugh, saying that she was the first visitor ever to refuse it. By the end of the summer, Rui had grown more confident. \"I'm still quiet,\" she says, \"but now I know my voice matters.\""},"27":{"w":["irresponsible","irretrievably","irritation","islander","isle","isolation","jade","jargon","judgment","juvenile","knowledgeable","kungfu","labor","laborious","lacquer","lacrosse","landmark","landowner","laptop","lateness"],"title":"The Kungfu School on the Isle","t":"On a small **isle** off the coast of Fujian, there is a **kungfu** school that has trained students for more than a century. Its stone gate has become a famous **landmark**, and visitors often take photos there. The school was founded by a wealthy **landowner** who believed that martial arts built both body and character.\n\nLife on the island is simple. Its **isolation** means that students must focus completely on training. Most of them are **juvenile** boys and girls sent by parents who hope they will become more disciplined. Training is **laborious**: students rise at five, run along the beach and practise for hours. Physical **labor**, such as carrying water and repairing walls, is also part of the daily routine.\n\nThe headmaster, Master Lin, is calm and **knowledgeable**. He avoids technical **jargon** and explains ideas in simple words. He believes that good **judgment** matters more than strength. \"A fighter who acts without thinking is **irresponsible**,\" he says. He punishes **lateness** strictly: students who arrive late must clean the courtyard.\n\nIslanders are proud of the school. Local craftsmen carve **jade** ornaments and make red **lacquer** boxes that students buy as gifts for their families. Every **islander** knows the students by name and treats them like family. Last year, a group of American students visited and taught the children to play **lacrosse**, while the children taught them basic kungfu moves.\n\nModern life has reached the island too. Master Lin now uses a **laptop** to manage the school's records, though he admits it causes him great **irritation**. Some traditions have been **irretrievably** lost, such as old training songs that no one recorded. But Master Lin is hopeful. \"The heart of kungfu is patience,\" he says. \"That will never disappear.\""},"28":{"w":["lavish","lawmaker","learner","legendary","legislative","legislature","legitimacy","lenient","levy","lifeless","lifelong","lifespan","lifestyle","limelight","linkage","livelihood","livestream","livestreaming","loess","logo"],"title":"Livestreaming from the Loess Plateau","t":"On the **Loess** Plateau in northern Shaanxi, where yellow hills stretch to the horizon, a young farmer named Gao Lei has become an internet star. Every evening, he hosts a **livestream** selling apples, millet and dried dates from his village. Thousands of viewers watch him, and his sales have transformed the **livelihood** of dozens of families.\n\nGao never expected to enter the **limelight**. He grew up herding sheep and was always a slow **learner** at school. After working in a city factory for five years, he returned home because the village was dying. Farming on the soft **loess** soil was difficult, and many fields had become **lifeless** after years of erosion.\n\nHe started **livestreaming** with a cheap phone and a homemade **logo** painted on a piece of cardboard. His honesty attracted viewers. He showed his real **lifestyle**: early mornings, hard work and simple meals. Soon, he became something of a **legendary** figure in the region, and journalists began to visit.\n\nHis success caught the attention of a provincial **lawmaker**, who invited him to speak to the **legislature** about rural development. Gao argued for **legislative** changes to help small farmers, such as being **lenient** with online sellers and reducing the tax **levy** on farm products. Some critics questioned the **legitimacy** of internet sellers, but Gao insisted that his products were real.\n\nGao is careful not to live a **lavish** life. He reinvests most of his income in the village, and he has built a **linkage** between farmers and city buyers. He has also planted trees to protect the soil, which could extend the **lifespan** of the farmland. \"This is my **lifelong** mission,\" he says."},"29":{"w":["loneliness","longitudinal","loom","lure","lyric","magical","mainstream","maize","majestic","mandate","mandatory","maneuver","manipulative","manufacturing","markedly","marketable","marketer","marketing","marketplace","markup"],"title":"The Corn Festival","t":"Every autumn, a mountain village in Guizhou holds a festival to celebrate the **maize** harvest. The village **marketplace** fills with stalls selling corn cakes, roasted cobs and handwoven cloth made on a traditional wooden **loom**. Behind the village, **majestic** mountains rise into the clouds, and the evening light makes everything look **magical**.\n\nThe festival has changed **markedly** in recent years. It began as a small local event, but a clever **marketer** from the county tourism office saw its potential. Through social media **marketing**, the festival has entered the **mainstream**, attracting thousands of visitors from the cities. Local products have become far more **marketable**, and farmers can now sell their corn at a higher **markup**.\n\nNot everyone is happy. Some villagers worry that tourism will **lure** young people away from farming. Others dislike the large **manufacturing** companies that now produce cheap souvenirs. A **longitudinal** study by a university has followed the village for ten years and found that incomes have risen, but so has **loneliness** among older residents whose children have left.\n\nThe government has introduced new rules. A provincial **mandate** requires that all festival food meet safety standards, and it is now **mandatory** for stall owners to obtain licences. Some businessmen have tried every **maneuver** to avoid the rules, and one was accused of being **manipulative** in his dealings with farmers.\n\nThe highlight of the festival is a singing contest. Women in bright costumes sing folk songs whose **lyrics|lyric** describe love, harvest and mountains. As the competition approaches, excitement begins to **loom** over the whole village. Visitors say the festival is unforgettable."},"30":{"w":["martial","materialize","maximize","medication","memorable","mentality","mentor","metabolism","metaphor","meter","methodology","meticulous","metro","micro","mid","midlife","migration","milestone","mindfulness","mindlessly"],"title":"A Midlife Change","t":"At forty-five, Mr. Song faced a **midlife** crisis. He had worked for twenty years as an engineer, spending long hours in an office and taking the **metro** home late every night. His health was poor; his doctor warned that his **metabolism** had slowed and that he needed **medication** for high blood pressure. \"I felt like a machine,\" he says.\n\nThe change began when a friend introduced him to **martial** arts. Mr. Song joined a small class led by an elderly **mentor**, Master Ho, who taught him not only movement but also **mindfulness**. \"Most people live **mindlessly**,\" Master Ho told him. \"They eat, work and sleep without noticing anything.\"\n\nMr. Song approached training with the same **meticulous** care he used in engineering. He kept detailed notes, measuring his progress in every **micro** detail of his movements. He even developed a **methodology** for tracking his heart rate and breathing. Master Ho laughed at his notebooks but admired his dedication.\n\nOver time, his **mentality** changed. He began to see life through a new **metaphor**: a river that flows around obstacles instead of fighting them. He started walking to work, counting every **meter**, and his blood pressure fell. In **mid**-June, he reached an important **milestone**: he passed his first martial arts exam.\n\nMr. Song now teaches beginners on weekends. He says the most **memorable** moment came when his own son joined the class. He hopes to **maximize** the time he has with his family. Each spring, they watch the **migration** of birds over the lake near their home. \"Dreams can **materialize** at any age,\" he says. \"You just have to start.\""},"31":{"w":["mindset","mini","miscalculate","misconception","misguided","mishandle","misrepresent","mistreatment","modernise","modernity","modernization","monastery","monolingual","monotonous","moody","mooncake","moonlight","morale","mortality","motherhood"],"title":"Mid-Autumn at the Monastery","t":"Last Mid-Autumn Festival, I spent three days at a Buddhist **monastery** in the mountains of Zhejiang. I had expected silence and **monotonous** routines, but the experience was full of surprises. On the festival night, the monks shared **mooncakes|mooncake** with visitors in the courtyard, and we sat together under the **moonlight**.\n\nI arrived with a **misconception**. I believed that monks rejected **modernity** completely, living as people did a thousand years ago. In fact, the monastery had begun to **modernise** in many ways: solar panels powered the kitchen, and a young monk managed bookings online. The abbot explained that **modernization** was not the enemy of tradition. \"The **mindset** matters more than the tools,\" he said.\n\nThe monks told me about the region's history. Decades ago, poverty was severe, and infant **mortality** was high. The monastery ran a small clinic, and the abbot's mother, who had experienced the hardships of **motherhood** in those years, volunteered there. Today, the village is healthier, and the clinic has become a **mini** hospital.\n\nNot all visitors behave well. Some tourists are noisy or **moody**, complaining about simple food and early mornings. A few journalists have tried to **misrepresent** the monastery as a business, and one reporter published a **misguided** article claiming that monks lived in luxury. The abbot laughed it off. \"People often **miscalculate** what makes us happy,\" he said.\n\nThe monastery also cares for animals. Monks rescue dogs and cats that have suffered **mistreatment**, and they never **mishandle** even a small insect. The abbot is fluent in three languages and teaches **monolingual** visitors a few words of Sanskrit. When I left, I felt calm and grateful. The visit had lifted my **morale** more than any holiday."},"32":{"w":["motivational","motivator","multicultural","multifaceted","multinational","municipality","nationalist","navigate","needy","neighbor","neuroscience","neuroscientist","neutrality","newborn","nomad","nomadic","non","nonconformity","nonprofit","noteworthy"],"title":"Mapping the Nomad Brain","t":"Dr. Sarah Okoro is a **neuroscientist** who studies how people **navigate** the world. Her latest project took her to the grasslands of Mongolia, where she worked with **nomadic** herding families. \"**Nomads|nomad** have an extraordinary sense of direction,\" she explains. \"They can cross hundreds of kilometres without maps.\"\n\nHer research in **neuroscience** focuses on the parts of the brain that store spatial memory. Using portable equipment, her team measured brain activity while herders found their way across the steppe. The results were **noteworthy**: experienced herders showed stronger activity in memory regions than city dwellers.\n\nThe project was supported by a **nonprofit** organisation that works with **needy** rural families. In exchange for participation, the team provided medical checks, including care for every **newborn** in the camps. Dr. Okoro insisted on scientific **neutrality**, avoiding political debates about land rights. She also worked hard to explain her research to **non**-scientists in simple language.\n\nHer team was **multicultural**, with members from Nigeria, China, Germany and Mongolia. Dr. Okoro says that a **multifaceted** team brings better ideas. She praised one Mongolian assistant as the project's greatest **motivator**, and his **motivational** speeches kept everyone going during cold nights. Some local **nationalist** groups were suspicious of foreign scientists at first, but trust grew over time.\n\nDr. Okoro has always valued **nonconformity**. She turned down a well-paid job at a **multinational** technology company to continue her research. She now lives in a small apartment owned by the **municipality**, and her elderly **neighbor** waters her plants when she travels. \"The brain is the most amazing map in the world,\" she says. \"I'm still learning to read it.\""},"33":{"w":["nutritional","nutritionist","nutritious","obesity","observational","observatory","obsessive","odds","offender","offense","ongoing","online","onset","openness","opposition","opt","optimize","ordeal","organisational","organizer"],"title":"The School Nutritionist","t":"Ms. He is a **nutritionist** who works with primary schools in a large southern city. Her mission is to fight childhood **obesity**, which has risen sharply in recent years. \"The **odds** of a child becoming overweight have doubled in a decade,\" she says. \"This is an **ongoing** crisis.\"\n\nHer work began with an **observational** study. She visited dozens of school canteens and recorded what children actually ate. The results were worrying: many meals had little **nutritional** value, and sugary snacks were everywhere. Schools that allowed vending machines were the worst **offenders|offender**, and some parents took **offense** when she said so publicly.\n\nMs. He faced strong **opposition** from food companies, which feared losing sales. She also met resistance from school managers, who worried about the cost of change. But she was persistent. She worked as the chief **organizer** of a city-wide campaign, bringing together doctors, teachers and parents. Her **organisational** skills impressed everyone.\n\nThe campaign aimed to **optimize** school menus without increasing costs. Schools began serving **nutritious** meals with more vegetables and less sugar. Parents could **opt** for healthier lunch packages through an **online** system, and the response was enthusiastic. Ms. He encouraged **openness**, publishing all menus and test results.\n\nThe experience was a personal **ordeal** at times. She received angry messages, and her husband, an astronomer at the city **observatory**, worried about her health. She admits that she can be **obsessive** about details. But the results speak for themselves: fewer children now show signs of early **onset** diabetes. \"Changing habits is slow,\" she says, \"but children deserve the best start.\""},"34":{"w":["originality","outdate","outfit","outperform","outrageous","outreach","outright","outweigh","overabundance","overburden","overhaul","overindulge","overprice","override","oversee","overturn","overuse","overview","overwork","pagoda"],"title":"Restoring the Pagoda","t":"On a hill above the river stands a nine-storey brick **pagoda** that is more than eight hundred years old. For decades, it was neglected: plants grew from its cracks, and the top floors were closed to visitors. Two years ago, the city decided on a complete **overhaul** of the building.\n\nThe project was led by an architect named Mr. Su, who was chosen to **oversee** the work because of his reputation for **originality**. He began with an **overview** of the pagoda's history, studying old drawings and photographs. He discovered that earlier repairs, using **outdated|outdate** methods, had actually damaged the structure.\n\nMr. Su faced many challenges. The budget was tight, and some suppliers tried to **overprice** materials. One company's prices were so **outrageous** that he rejected its offer **outright**. He refused to **overuse** modern cement, which could crack the old bricks, and he insisted on traditional lime mortar. \"The benefits of authenticity **outweigh** the extra cost,\" he argued.\n\nNot everyone agreed. A city official tried to **override** his decisions to save money, and at one point it seemed the council might **overturn** the whole plan. Mr. Su's team worked long hours, and he worried that he might **overburden** them. \"I told them not to **overwork**,\" he says, \"but they loved the project too much.\"\n\nToday, the pagoda is open again. Its restoration has helped it **outperform** other tourist sites in the region. The city runs an **outreach** programme inviting schoolchildren to learn about ancient architecture. Visitors are warned not to **overindulge** in snacks sold at the bottom of the hill, where an **overabundance** of stalls has appeared. Mr. Su still wears the same work **outfit** he wore on the first day: a blue jacket and a hard hat."},"35":{"w":["pandemic","paper-cut","papermaking","paradigm","paradoxical","parasite","parental","parenting","parity","participation","partnership","passionate","password","pathway","patriotism","pavilion","paycheck","perfectionism","perfectionist","perishable"],"title":"The Paper-Cut Artist","t":"Grandma Zhao is a famous **paper-cut** artist from a village in Shaanxi. Using only scissors and red paper, she creates intricate designs of flowers, animals and scenes from village life. Her work is displayed in a **pavilion** at the provincial museum, and visitors are amazed by its detail.\n\nShe learned the craft as a child from her mother, who also taught her the history of **papermaking** in China. Grandma Zhao describes herself as a **perfectionist**; she sometimes spends weeks on a single piece. Her **perfectionism** can be frustrating for her students, but they admire her standards.\n\nHer life has not been easy. As a young woman, she raised five children while working in the fields. She describes **parenting** in those years as a constant struggle, with little **parental** support from the government. \"Our **paycheck** was a bag of rice,\" she jokes. She sees a **paradoxical** truth in her story: poverty made her stronger and more creative.\n\nToday, Grandma Zhao is **passionate** about passing on her skills. During the **pandemic**, when schools closed, she taught classes online, though she always forgot her **password**. Her granddaughter now runs the classes, and **participation** has grown enormously. The village has formed a **partnership** with a university, creating a new **pathway** for young people to study traditional art.\n\nShe believes that folk art deserves **parity** with fine art. \"Both come from the heart,\" she says. Her designs often express **patriotism** and love for her homeland. One of her favourite works shows farmers protecting their crops from a **parasite** that once destroyed the harvest. Some scholars describe her work as a new **paradigm** for rural art. Her paper is fragile and **perishable**, but she hopes her ideas will last forever."},"36":{"w":["perpetuate","perseverance","personalize","persuasive","pesticide","pharmaceutical","pharmacy","phd","philosophical","phoenix","phonic","piety","pizza","placement","plagiarism","planetary","planner","planter","plasticity","playful"],"title":"The Pharmacist's Daughter","t":"My friend Li Na grew up above her parents' small **pharmacy** in a quiet town in Hunan. As a child, she loved watching her father prepare medicines and listening to customers describe their problems. Today, she is finishing a **PhD** in chemistry, and her research focuses on safer alternatives to the **pesticide** chemicals used on farms.\n\nHer path was not easy. She failed her first university entrance exam, but her **perseverance** paid off the following year. During her studies, she completed a **placement** at a large **pharmaceutical** company, where she learned how new drugs are tested. She says the experience showed her both the power and the responsibility of science.\n\nLi Na is deeply **philosophical** about her work. She believes that science must protect the health of the planet as a whole, not just individuals. \"Our problems are **planetary**,\" she says. \"Pesticides travel through water and air, far beyond the fields.\" She hopes her research will help stop farming methods that **perpetuate** pollution.\n\nShe is also a natural teacher. At weekends, she tutors primary school children, using **phonic** games to help them read English. She tries to **personalize** each lesson and encourages a **playful** spirit in class. \"Children's brains have amazing **plasticity**,\" she explains. \"They learn best when they're having fun.\"\n\nLi Na takes academic honesty seriously. When a classmate was caught committing **plagiarism**, she was shocked and upset. Her own writing is clear and **persuasive**, and her supervisor calls her a born scientist. She shows great **piety** towards her parents, returning home every holiday. Her father keeps a **planter** of herbs on the windowsill and a **planner** full of customers' birthdays. When she finishes her degree, the family plans to celebrate with **pizza**, her favourite food. Her mother compares her to a **phoenix**: \"She rose from failure and flew higher.\""},"37":{"w":["plaza","pleasurable","plight","plumber","poetic","poise","polarization","policymaker","pollutant","popularize","populate","portrayal","positivity","postmaster","potent","pottery","practitioner","pragmatic","praiseworthy","predator"],"title":"The Pottery Market","t":"Every Sunday, the central **plaza** of our old town fills with stalls selling handmade **pottery**. Bowls, vases and teapots in every colour line the square, and craftsmen work at their wheels while visitors watch. For many tourists, browsing the market is the most **pleasurable** part of their trip.\n\nThe market was founded by a retired **postmaster**, Mr. Dong, who loved pottery and wanted to help local artists. Twenty years ago, their **plight** was serious: cheap factory goods had nearly destroyed traditional crafts. Mr. Dong persuaded the town's **policymakers|policymaker** to provide space for a weekly market. His **pragmatic** approach and quiet **positivity** won everyone's support.\n\nThe craftsmen are a colourful group. One potter, a former **plumber**, says that working with clay feels like working with pipes, but more **poetic**. Another, an experienced **practitioner** of a thousand-year-old glazing method, has helped to **popularize** the technique among young artists. Visitors admire the **poise** with which she lifts delicate pieces from the kiln.\n\nThe market has faced challenges. A nearby factory once released a **pollutant** into the river, damaging the clay beds where potters collect their materials. The issue caused **polarization** in the town: some residents defended the factory jobs, while others demanded its closure. Eventually, the factory installed filters, a decision many called **praiseworthy**.\n\nThe area around the plaza has become more densely **populated|populate**, and new cafés have opened. A local film offered a warm **portrayal** of the market, and its message about community proved **potent**. Mr. Dong compares big companies to a **predator** that hunts small businesses. \"But together,\" he says, \"we survive.\""},"38":{"w":["predictable","prehistoric","preoccupy","preschool","preschooler","prestigious","preteen","prioritize","pro","problematic","programmer","progression","proofread","prosecutor","prosper","protectionist","protector","provider","psychologist","purist"],"title":"A Programmer's Family","t":"Mr. Lu is a **programmer** at a technology company in Shenzhen. He works long hours, but his real passion is his family. He has two children: a **preschooler** who loves dinosaurs and a **preteen** daughter who wants to become a **prosecutor**. Balancing work and family is his constant challenge.\n\nHis younger son attends a **preschool** near the office, and Mr. Lu picks him up every afternoon. On weekends, they visit the city museum, where the boy stares at **prehistoric** skeletons for hours. \"He's completely **preoccupied|preoccupy** with dinosaurs,\" Mr. Lu laughs. His daughter, meanwhile, studies hard, hoping to attend a **prestigious** law school one day.\n\nA family **psychologist** once advised Mr. Lu to **prioritize** time with his children over overtime at work. He found the advice difficult but important. He now lists the **pros|pro** and cons of every extra project before agreeing to it. Long working hours, he admits, are **problematic** for many families in his industry.\n\nMr. Lu sees himself as both a **provider** and a **protector**. He worries about the **progression** of technology and its effect on children. He limits screen time, though he confesses he is not a **purist**: the children are allowed one cartoon every evening. He also helps his daughter **proofread** her essays, correcting her grammar and spelling.\n\nHis company has faced difficulties. Trade disputes and **protectionist** policies in some countries have made exports harder, and growth has become less **predictable**. But Mr. Lu remains optimistic. \"Good families and good companies **prosper** for the same reason,\" he says. \"They care about people.\""},"39":{"w":["qualifier","quinoa","quitter","radiance","rationality","readiness","rebound","receptive","recharge","recollection","reconstruct","recreate","recreational","recruiter","rectification","redefine","reexamine","reforestation","regain","regional"],"title":"The Forest Comes Back","t":"Twenty years ago, the hills around my hometown were bare and brown, the result of decades of logging. Today, they are covered with young pine and oak trees, thanks to a **regional** **reforestation** programme. The project has helped the land **regain** its health and brought birds and animals back to the valley.\n\nMy uncle, a forestry officer, has worked on the project from the start. His earliest **recollection** of the hills is of dust storms that blew across the fields every spring. \"We had to **redefine** our relationship with nature,\" he says. \"We had to **reexamine** everything we believed about progress.\"\n\nThe programme required careful planning. Scientists worked to **reconstruct** the original forest, studying old records to **recreate** its mixture of species. A government **rectification** campaign closed illegal logging companies, and a **recruiter** hired hundreds of local workers to plant trees. At first, villagers were not **receptive** to the idea, but their **readiness** to help grew when they saw the results.\n\nThe forest has also become a place for **recreational** activities. Families walk the trails at weekends, and city workers come to **recharge** their energy. The local economy has begun to **rebound**, with new guesthouses and cafés. A farmer who once grew corn now grows **quinoa** for health-food shops.\n\nMy uncle believes that **rationality** and patience saved the land. \"Some people said we'd fail,\" he says. \"But I'm not a **quitter**.\" His cousin, a long-distance runner, trains on the forest paths and recently became a national **qualifier** for the marathon. On autumn mornings, sunlight fills the valley with a soft **radiance**, and my uncle smiles at the hills he helped to save."},"40":{"w":["regulatory","rehabilitation","reinforcement","reintroduce","related","relaxation","relentless","reliant","relic","reminder","renewable","renewal","renovate","renowned","rental","repetitive","reportedly","reproductive","reputable","resentful"],"title":"The Stork Rescue Centre","t":"On the edge of a wetland in eastern China, a small centre carries out the **rehabilitation** of injured birds, especially the rare oriental stork. Its founder, Dr. Han, is a **renowned** biologist who has spent thirty years protecting the species. The centre has **reportedly** saved more than five hundred birds.\n\nThe work is **relentless**. Volunteers feed the birds several times a day, clean their cages and record their health. The tasks are **repetitive**, but everyone understands their importance. Dr. Han's team also studies the storks' **reproductive** habits, hoping to increase their numbers in the wild.\n\nThe centre's greatest success came last year, when it helped **reintroduce** twenty storks into a protected wetland. Government **regulatory** agencies supported the project, and a **reputable** international charity provided funding. A group of volunteers from a **related** organisation acted as **reinforcement** during the busy spring season.\n\nThe centre itself was once an abandoned farmhouse. Volunteers worked for months to **renovate** it, installing **renewable** energy systems such as solar panels and a small wind turbine. The building's **rental** contract is due for **renewal** next year, and Dr. Han hopes the owner will offer a lower price. In the garden, volunteers found an old stone **relic**, which now sits by the entrance as a **reminder** of the farm's long history.\n\nNot everyone supports the centre. Some local farmers are **resentful** because storks eat fish from their ponds. Dr. Han meets them regularly to find solutions. She believes that the wetland should not remain **reliant** on human help forever. For **relaxation**, she sits by the water at sunset, watching the storks return home."},"41":{"w":["reshape","resourceful","respondent","responsive","restoration","restructure","rethink","retrain","retrieval","reunite","revisit","revitalization","revitalize","revival","revolutionize","reword","rhetoric","riches","rigor","risky"],"title":"Bringing an Old Town Back to Life","t":"Ten years ago, the old mining town of Pingshan seemed to have no future. The mines had closed, young people had left, and the main street was full of empty shops. Today, it is one of the most popular tourist destinations in the province. Its **revitalization** shows how careful planning can **reshape** a community.\n\nThe change began when the town's new mayor decided to **rethink** its economy completely. She commissioned a survey of residents, and every **respondent** was asked what they valued most about the town. The answers were clear: history, nature and community. The mayor's plan was to **revitalize** the town around these strengths.\n\nThe work was **risky**. The council had to **restructure** its budget and borrow money to fund the **restoration** of old buildings. Former miners were invited to **retrain** as guides, builders and craftsmen. A **resourceful** group of retired teachers organised the **retrieval** of old photographs and documents from family homes, creating a museum of the town's history.\n\nThe mayor avoided empty **rhetoric**. When she presented her plans, she spoke simply and honestly, and she was **responsive** to criticism. Once, after residents complained that her proposal was too complicated, she agreed to **reword** it in plain language. Her team applied scientific **rigor** to every decision, measuring results carefully.\n\nThe results have been remarkable. Tourism has helped to **revolutionize** the local economy, and a **revival** of traditional crafts has created new jobs. Many families who left years ago have returned, and several have been able to **reunite** with relatives they had not seen for a decade. Visitors who **revisit** the town are amazed by the changes. \"We didn't find **riches**,\" the mayor says. \"We found ourselves.\""},"42":{"w":["roadside","robotic","roommate","ruinous","rum","runoff","sales","sameness","sarcasm","sarcastic","sarcastically","scanner","scary","scenario","scenic","scroll","scrutinize","seafood","seeker","selective"],"title":"The Road Trip","t":"Last summer, my **roommate** Kai and I took a road trip along the southern coast. We had planned the route carefully, choosing the most **scenic** roads and booking cheap hotels. Kai is famous for his **sarcasm**, and he joked that our old car would never survive the journey.\n\nThe first days were wonderful. We stopped at every **roadside** stall selling fresh fruit and grilled **seafood**, and we watched the sunset from cliffs above the sea. One evening, a bar owner offered us glasses of local **rum**, which tasted like burnt sugar. Kai is **selective** about food, but even he admitted it was delicious.\n\nThen things went wrong. On the third day, heavy rain caused **runoff** from the mountains to flood the road, and we had to wait for hours. The car began to make **scary** noises, and the repair bill was almost **ruinous** for two students. Kai said **sarcastically** that it was \"the best holiday ever.\" I couldn't help laughing at his **sarcastic** tone, even though I was worried.\n\nAt the garage, a mechanic used a **robotic** arm and a computer **scanner** to diagnose the problem. He explained the worst-case **scenario**: the engine might need replacing. While we waited, Kai would **scroll** through his phone, looking for cheaper solutions. I tried to **scrutinize** the bill, but I understood nothing.\n\nFortunately, the problem was small. As we drove on, Kai noticed the **sameness** of the new hotels along the coast; each one looked identical. The receptionist at one told us that **sales** of holiday packages had fallen because tourists wanted something different. \"We're adventure **seekers|seeker**,\" Kai told her proudly. \"We just want our car to work.\""},"43":{"w":["selectively","semaphore","sensational","sensory","sentimental","sequel","seriousness","severity","showcase","shyness","sidewalk","signaling","silkworm","simplistic","simulation","sinful","slash","sleeper","slump","smallness"],"title":"The Silk Museum","t":"In the city of Hangzhou, a small museum tells the story of silk, from the tiny **silkworm** to the finest fabric in the world. Its main hall is a **showcase** of ancient robes, embroidered shoes and painted fans. Visitors often describe the experience as a **sensory** journey: they can touch raw silk, smell mulberry leaves and listen to the sound of old looms.\n\nThe museum's director, Ms. Xu, is a quiet woman whose **shyness** disappears when she talks about silk. She grew up in a family of silk weavers and has a **sentimental** attachment to the craft. She dislikes **simplistic** stories about history and insists that the museum show the hard work of ordinary weavers, not just the luxury of emperors.\n\nOne room describes how silk was traded along ancient routes. A **simulation** allows visitors to \"travel\" by camel across deserts, and a display explains how merchants used flags and **semaphore** for **signaling** between caravans. Another room explores the **severity** of working conditions in early factories, treating the subject with **seriousness**.\n\nThe museum has faced difficulties. During an economic **slump**, the government had to **slash** its budget, and Ms. Xu worried it might close. A **sensational** news story claimed that the museum was selling fake antiques, but investigators proved it false. She admits that she reads such reports only **selectively** now.\n\nToday, the museum is thriving. Tourists arrive by overnight **sleeper** train from Beijing, and the **sidewalk** outside is often crowded. A popular documentary about the museum is getting a **sequel**. Old weavers once believed it was **sinful** to waste even a thread of silk. Ms. Xu says that the **smallness** of each silkworm makes its achievement all the more remarkable."},"44":{"w":["smartphone","snobbery","sociable","socialize","societal","socioeconomic","sociologist","solicit","solicitor","solitude","solstice","someday","sooner","southwestern","spa","sparingly","specialty","speculation","speedily","spending"],"title":"Life Through a Smartphone","t":"Dr. Pan is a **sociologist** who studies how the **smartphone** has changed daily life in China. Her research covers people from different **socioeconomic** backgrounds, from wealthy city professionals to farmers in remote **southwestern** villages. \"Phones have transformed every **societal** institution,\" she says, \"from families to markets.\"\n\nHer findings are surprising. Many people use phones to **socialize** more than ever, joining online groups and sharing photos. But Dr. Pan has also found that some **sociable** people feel lonelier than before. Others actively seek **solitude**, turning off their phones on weekends or visiting a mountain **spa** to escape notifications.\n\nDr. Pan studies **spending** habits too. Online shopping has made buying things easier, and many young people spend money **speedily** and without thinking. Advertising apps **solicit** personal data in exchange for discounts. She advises people to use apps **sparingly** and to read privacy rules carefully. A **solicitor** she interviewed warned that many users don't understand the contracts they accept.\n\nHer research has revealed a new kind of **snobbery**: some people judge others by the brand of phone they carry. Dr. Pan's **specialty** is the study of rural communities, where phones have helped farmers sell crops online. She dismisses **speculation** that technology will destroy village life. \"It's changing, not disappearing,\" she says.\n\nShe practises what she preaches. On the winter **solstice** each year, she spends a day without any devices, walking in the countryside. \"**Someday**, we will learn to balance technology and life,\" she says. \"The **sooner** we start, the better.\""},"45":{"w":["spicy","spire","splendour","spokesperson","sponsorship","sprawl","staffer","stagnation","staple","starter","startup","statutory","stifle","stigma","stimulation","stipulate","stressful","stuffiness","stump","sub"],"title":"The Hotpot Startup","t":"Three years ago, two friends from Chengdu launched a **startup** selling ready-to-cook **spicy** hotpot kits online. Their idea was simple: bring the taste of Sichuan to busy office workers across the country. Today, the company employs eighty people and sells millions of kits every year.\n\nThe founders, Xiao Ma and Xiao Liu, started with almost nothing. They rented a small kitchen with no windows, and its **stuffiness** in summer was almost unbearable. Early months were **stressful**: they worked through the night, and some days they barely sold anything. Hard questions from investors would often **stump** them, and Xiao Liu's mother was sure the business would never grow.\n\nTheir big break came through a **sponsorship** deal with a popular online cooking show. The company's **spokesperson**, a cheerful **staffer** named Lili, became famous for her funny videos. Sales grew rapidly, and the kits became a **staple** in many young people's kitchens. Each box includes a small **starter** dish of pickled vegetables.\n\nThe company has faced challenges. New government rules **stipulate** strict standards for food packaging, and meeting these **statutory** requirements was expensive. The founders worry that too many regulations could **stifle** innovation. They also fought the **stigma** that instant food is unhealthy, proving that their kits used fresh ingredients.\n\nTheir headquarters is now in a modern building in the city's eastern **sprawl**, with a view of an old temple **spire**. The founders believe that economic **stagnation** is not inevitable; creativity provides the **stimulation** that growth needs. On weekends, they play basketball, and Xiao Ma usually comes in as a **sub** for the second half. \"Success is not about **splendour**,\" he says. \"It's about good food and good friends.\""},"46":{"w":["submissive","subset","subsidize","subtlety","supercomputing","superintendent","supervisor","supplier","supporter","supportive","supposedly","surf","surgical","surveillance","susceptibility","sustainability","sustainable","sustainably","symbolic","symbolize"],"title":"The Green Supercomputer","t":"On the edge of a mountain city in Guizhou, there is a huge **supercomputing** centre hidden inside a cave. The cool air underground keeps the machines from overheating, and the centre is powered mainly by wind and water. Engineers say it is a model of **sustainability**: proof that advanced technology can be run **sustainably**.\n\nThe centre's director, Dr. Kong, is a **supportive** leader who encourages young engineers to share ideas. She began her career as a research **supervisor** at a university, and she still treats her staff like students. \"Every problem has a **subtlety** that only careful thinkers notice,\" she tells them.\n\nBuilding a **sustainable** centre was not easy. The government agreed to **subsidize** part of the cost, but the team had to find a **supplier** willing to provide recycled equipment. The building **superintendent** spent months checking cooling systems and safety rules. Some critics argued that the project was too expensive, but **supporters|supporter** insisted that it would pay for itself within ten years.\n\nThe supercomputer is used for many purposes. Doctors use it to design **surgical** robots, and scientists study the **susceptibility** of crops to climate change. Only a small **subset** of its power is used for commercial work. Dr. Kong firmly refuses to use it for mass **surveillance**, saying that technology must respect privacy.\n\nThe cave has become **symbolic** of the region's transformation. Guizhou was once one of China's poorest provinces, and the centre has come to **symbolize** its new confidence. Dr. Kong dislikes the old idea that rural regions must be **submissive** to big cities. She laughs at the claim that engineers spend their days **surfing|surf** the internet. \"**Supposedly** we have the easiest job in the world,\" she says. \"Come and try it.\""},"47":{"w":["syndrome","tactfully","tactic","talented","taoism","taxation","teahouse","teammate","tech","technological","teddy","teen","teenage","telecom","telecommunication","telegraphy","telemedicine","telomere","tenure","terraced"],"title":"The Teahouse on the Hill","t":"On a hillside covered with **terraced** tea fields in Fujian, there is a small **teahouse** run by an old man named Grandpa Xu. Visitors climb a narrow path to reach it, and they are rewarded with fresh tea and a view of green hills. Grandpa Xu is a follower of **Taoism**, and he believes that tea teaches patience and balance.\n\nThe teahouse has become popular with young people. A group of **teen** hikers recently stopped there, and one **teenage** girl, a **talented** photographer, took pictures that went viral online. She and her **teammate** from a school hiking club have since returned many times. One of them always carries a small **teddy** bear as a lucky charm.\n\nGrandpa Xu's grandson works in **tech**, and he has brought some **technological** changes to the teahouse. A new **telecom** tower on the next hill now provides internet access, and **telecommunication** has transformed village life. During a recent illness, Grandpa Xu used **telemedicine** to talk with a doctor in the city. He jokes that, as a boy, the only fast communication he knew was **telegraphy** at the county post office.\n\nHe handles difficult customers **tactfully**. When tourists complain about prices, he explains how **taxation** and transport costs affect small businesses. His favourite **tactic** is to offer a free second cup, which usually ends any argument.\n\nGrandpa Xu enjoys reading about science. He once read an article about **telomere** length and ageing, and he laughs that tea must be good for his. Doctors say he shows no sign of any age-related **syndrome**. He has held the **tenure** of the teahouse for fifty years, and he has no plans to stop."},"48":{"w":["terracotta","theatrical","theorist","therapist","thorny","tidiness","timer","toll","tonne","tranquility","transformative","transparency","traumatic","traveling","trendy","tribal","tributary","tricky","trillion","trustworthy"],"title":"Soldiers of Clay","t":"Last year, a **traveling** exhibition of **terracotta** warriors from Xi'an toured several European cities. The ancient clay soldiers, each more than two thousand years old, attracted huge crowds. Many visitors described the experience as **transformative**: standing face to face with the warriors made history feel real.\n\nTransporting the statues was a **tricky** task. Each figure weighs hundreds of kilograms, and some of the horses weigh almost a **tonne**. Experts packed them carefully in special boxes, and a **timer** recorded every moment the statues were outside controlled conditions. The insurance value of the collection was estimated in the hundreds of millions, though one journalist exaggerated it to a **trillion** yuan.\n\nThe exhibition raised some **thorny** questions. Critics asked about the **transparency** of agreements between museums, and others debated whether ancient treasures should travel at all. A historian and **theorist** of museum studies argued that sharing culture builds trust between nations. The museum also had to be **trustworthy**, keeping careful records of every object.\n\nThe display itself was designed with **theatrical** lighting, creating shadows that made the soldiers seem alive. Some visitors found it too **trendy**, preferring the calm **tranquility** of traditional museums. The curators were famous for their **tidiness**, and every label was perfectly aligned.\n\nThe exhibition also explored history's darker side. Thousands of workers built the emperor's tomb, and many died; the **toll** was enormous. A **therapist** who visited said the stories of forced labour were **traumatic** for some visitors. The exhibition compared the emperor's armies with **tribal** warriors in other cultures. One map showed the Yellow River and each **tributary** that fed the ancient capital."},"49":{"w":["truthful","tweet","ultra","unacceptable","unaffordable","unanticipated","unaware","uncanny","uncertainty","unclear","uncommon","uncontrollable","uncontrolled","uncritical","undeniably","underlie","underprivileged","underrepresented","undervalue","underway"],"title":"A Chance for Every Student","t":"Five years ago, a university in Beijing launched a programme to help **underprivileged** students from rural areas. These students are **underrepresented** at top universities, where most places go to children from wealthy city families. The programme, now well **underway**, has helped hundreds of young people achieve their dreams.\n\nIts founder, Professor Gu, says that the problem is **undeniably** serious. Many talented rural students are **unaware** of opportunities, and university fees can be **unaffordable** for poor families. \"Schools often **undervalue** students who speak with a rural accent,\" she says. \"That is **unacceptable**.\"\n\nThe programme has faced **uncertainty** from the start. Funding was **unclear** in the first year, and some officials were **uncritical** supporters of the old system. **Unanticipated** problems also appeared: some students felt lonely and struggled with **uncontrollable** homesickness. The programme added counselling services to help them adjust.\n\nProfessor Gu insists on being **truthful** about the challenges. She once posted a **tweet** criticising **ultra**-competitive exam culture, and it was shared thousands of times. Some critics accused her of encouraging **uncontrolled** admissions, but she replied calmly that standards remained high.\n\nThe success stories are inspiring. One student, the first in her village to attend university, now studies medicine. She has an **uncanny** ability to remember every detail of her textbooks. Such talent is not **uncommon**, Professor Gu says; it simply needs a chance. She believes that fairness should **underlie** every education policy."},"50":{"w":["undesired","undeveloped","undisguised","unequal","unerring","unexcused","unfamiliar","unfavorable","unhappiness","unheard","unhelpful","unintended","unintentionally","unleash","unpopular","unpredictable","unprocessed","unrealistic","unrelated","unrestricted"],"title":"The New Principal","t":"When Ms. Zhao became principal of a struggling secondary school, she found many problems. **Unexcused** absences were common, students' **unhappiness** was obvious, and teachers felt their opinions went **unheard**. The school had **unequal** resources: some classrooms had new computers, while others lacked basic textbooks.\n\nMs. Zhao was **unfamiliar** with the town, but she had an **unerring** sense of what students needed. Her first decisions were **unpopular**. She banned **unrestricted** phone use during lessons and replaced **unprocessed** snacks in the canteen with healthier meals. Some parents expressed **undisguised** anger, and a few teachers made **unhelpful** comments at meetings.\n\nShe knew that change can have **unintended** consequences. When she introduced longer school days, some students became more tired, an **undesired** effect she had not expected. She **unintentionally** upset the sports teachers by cutting their training time, and she quickly apologised and changed the schedule.\n\nHer boldest idea was to turn a piece of **undeveloped** land beside the school into a garden. Despite **unfavorable** weather in the first year, students grew vegetables and flowers. The project seemed to **unleash** their creativity and confidence. Even students who were often in trouble found something they loved.\n\nMs. Zhao admits that teaching is **unpredictable**. Some of her plans were **unrealistic**, and others failed for reasons **unrelated** to her decisions. But after three years, attendance has risen sharply and exam results have improved. \"Listening is the most important skill a principal can have,\" she says."},"51":{"w":["unscientific","unseen","unskilled","unsociable","unsold","unsolicited","unused","unwanted","unwelcome","unwillingness","upcoming","upload","upside","urbanise","urbanization","urgency","usable","vaccine","validate","validation"],"title":"The Second-Hand App","t":"As **urbanization** accelerates across China, millions of young people move into small city flats. Many arrive with little furniture and leave a few years later with piles of **unwanted** things. A young entrepreneur named Chen Bo saw an opportunity: an app where people can sell or give away **unused** items.\n\nThe idea came from personal experience. When Chen moved to Shanghai, he found that most of his neighbours were **unsociable**, rarely speaking to each other. He wondered whether sharing things could build community. The app lets users **upload** photos of old furniture, clothes and electronics, and buyers can collect them nearby. Every **usable** item gets a second life.\n\nThe project faced doubts. Investors showed **unwillingness** to fund it, and some called the idea **unscientific**, saying there was no proof that people would trust strangers. Chen had to **validate** his idea by testing it in three neighbourhoods. Their **validation** convinced investors, and the app soon spread across the city.\n\nThe app has an unexpected **upside**: it helps **unskilled** workers earn extra money by delivering and repairing goods. During a city **vaccine** campaign, clinics even used the app to find **unused** fridges for storing medicine. Chen says that **unsold** goods are the biggest **unseen** cost of modern consumption.\n\nNot every message is friendly. Users sometimes receive **unsolicited** advertisements, and some feel these are **unwelcome**. Chen's team works with great **urgency** to block spam. As cities continue to **urbanise**, he plans to expand to other regions. \"The **upcoming** generation,\" he says, \"cares more about sharing than owning.\""},"52":{"w":["variability","vegan","vegetarian","vend","ventilation","venue","verification","viability","viable","visionary","vitality","volatile","volcanic","voter","vulnerability","waiver","warranty","wary","wasteland","waterway"],"title":"The Vegan Food Festival","t":"Last summer, our city held its first **vegan** and **vegetarian** food festival. The **venue** was an old warehouse beside the river **waterway**, transformed with lights, plants and long wooden tables. More than twenty thousand people attended, surprising even the organisers.\n\nThe festival's founder, Ms. Lan, is a **visionary** who believes that plant-based food is the future. Ten years ago, people told her that a vegetarian business was not **viable**. Today, she runs five restaurants. \"People are **wary** of change at first,\" she says, \"but taste wins in the end.\"\n\nOrganising the festival involved many details. Each stall had to pass a **verification** process to confirm that its food contained no animal products. The old building needed better **ventilation**, so engineers installed large fans. Small businesses were offered a fee **waiver**, which allowed young cooks to **vend** their food for free. Equipment rental companies provided a **warranty** for every cooking machine.\n\nFood prices have been **volatile** in recent years, and there is great **variability** in the cost of vegetables. One farmer explained that his crops grow in rich **volcanic** soil, which produces sweet tomatoes. Another described how he had turned a **wasteland** into a farm through years of hard work. Experts discussed the economic **viability** of vegan farming and the **vulnerability** of small farmers to climate change.\n\nThe festival had a political side too. Local candidates came to speak with each **voter** about environmental policies. But most visitors simply came to eat. The atmosphere was full of energy and **vitality**, with music playing late into the night."},"53":{"w":["wearer","weaving","weird","wellbeing","wetland","wholesale","willpower","withdrawal","workload","workout","worldview","worrisome","worsen","worthiness","worthlessness","wow","yeah","yoga","yogurt","zodiac"],"title":"Finding Balance","t":"Last year, my friend Ling was exhausted. Her **workload** at a law firm had doubled, and she often worked until midnight. Her health began to **worsen**: she slept badly, ate junk food and stopped exercising. \"I felt a kind of **worthlessness**,\" she says. \"My job was all I had.\"\n\nThe turning point came during a holiday at a **wetland** park in the south. Watching birds float on the quiet water, she realised that her **worldview** had become too narrow. \"I thought success meant money,\" she says. \"I had forgotten about **wellbeing**.\"\n\nWhen she returned home, she made changes. She started a morning **workout** and joined a **yoga** class near her office. At first, the poses felt **weird**, and her muscles complained. \"**Wow**, this is harder than law,\" she laughed. Her teacher told her that **willpower** matters more than flexibility. Ling replaced her usual breakfast with **yogurt** and fruit.\n\nSome changes were difficult. She experienced a mild **withdrawal** from coffee, suffering headaches for a week. Her boss found her new schedule **worrisome**, but she argued that a healthier employee works better. \"**Yeah**, you were right,\" her boss later admitted.\n\nLing also discovered new hobbies. She took a class in **weaving** and made a scarf for her mother, and she now buys fabric **wholesale** to save money. Her mother, the scarf's proud **wearer**, believes in the Chinese **zodiac** and says that the year of the rabbit brought Ling good luck. Ling no longer measures her **worthiness** by her salary. \"Life is about balance,\" she says."}});
